# Cloudflare Pages `_redirects`
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 404 HTTP-status handling (blueprint §13.4 / gap #19)
#
# IMPORTANT — Cloudflare Pages DOES NOT support status-code rewrites to 404 in
# `_redirects`. Only 301 / 302 / 303 / 307 / 308 are accepted; a rule like
# `/* /404 404` is invalid and would be ignored (or break the fallback below).
#
# So the SPA fallback below is the best available: every unknown path is served
# index.html with HTTP 200. Known public routes are pre-rendered to static HTML
# by vike (pages/+onBeforePrerenderStart.ts, 37 routes) and serve real 200s.
# Only genuinely missing URLs (typos/stale links) return 200-instead-of-404 —
# this is the documented SPA limitation.
#
# noindex mitigation (applied): the on-brand 404 page
# (src/pages/public/not-found.tsx) renders <meta name="robots"
# content="noindex, nofollow"> via SeoHead, so stray/missing URLs never get
# indexed. Verified present — do not remove `noindex` from that page.
#
# If true HTTP-404 statuses become a requirement, switch to a Pages Function
# (e.g. an `_routes.json`-protected catch-all returning `Response 404`) — that
# is the only supported mechanism on Cloudflare Pages.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SPA fallback — must stay the LAST rule and keep the 200 status so app routing
# and pre-rendered pages are never broken.
/*    /index.html    200
